      "Synthetic nurse note for software testing. No real patient data.\n\nThe driver reports diabetes managed with tablets, but an older specialist letter describes diet and exercise alone and the current medication box is not available for verification; he says the tablets were changed last month and has not used insulin to his knowledge. During a late shift he became shaky and struggled to read a docket, then ate a snack without assistance and felt better, but no glucose was checked until nearly an hour later, when the meter displayed 6.2 mmol/L.\n\nHe denies having lost consciousness or needing another person to treat the episode, although a supervisor says a colleague took over the loading paperwork because he seemed confused. His home diary contains several blank days and a single 3.8 mmol/L reading with no time or symptom annotation, so the relationship between that entry and the workplace event cannot be determined.\n\nAt today's review BP 174/102 mmHg was followed after rest by BP 158/92 mmHg, and he says he missed a blood-pressure tablet that morning. There is no documented persistent reading, no verified four-week period of control and no recent specialist report; the nurse records possible glucose-related symptoms and variable pressure measurements without assigning a definite cause or choosing one reading as representative.\n"
